// A counter that wraps — and the one thing in this directory a value type takes *away*.
//
// SCTP numbers every DATA chunk with a 32-bit TSN that wraps. `packages/webrtc/src/sctp.wac` gets
// this right, and says why:
//
// > TSNs are unsigned 32-bit and **wrap**, so `a < b` on the numbers is wrong twice over: held in an
// > `i32` half of them are negative to begin with, and even unsigned the comparison inverts when a
// > sequence crosses 2^32. Subtracting and testing the sign is right in both cases, because the
// > subtraction wraps the same way the counter does.
//
//     export bool tsnBefore(i32 a, i32 b) { return (a - b) < 0; }
//
// Ten call sites on nine lines of one file, all correct — checked, and six of the ten are written
// `!tsnBefore(…)`. At every one of them **`a < b` was available and
// would have compiled**, on a value whose type says nothing about which comparison is the right one.
// The comment above exists because the wrong one is one character shorter.
//
// ## Every other finding here adds a check; this one removes an operator
//
// A `Rect`, a `Chunk`, a `Gindex`, a `Tile` — each of those makes something *checkable* that was
// not. This does the opposite: `Tsn` is a struct, and comparison operators are not defined on
// structs, so `a < b` on two `Tsn`s **does not compile at all**. The only ordering in scope is the
// one the RFC defines.
//
// That is worth naming because it generalises past this file. The reason `i32` is dangerous here is
// not that it is imprecise; it is that it comes with an ordering, and the ordering is wrong. Any
// wrapping counter has this shape: a sequence number, a version, a slot in a ring. Wrapping and the
// operators are two facts and the language attaches them to one type.

/**
 * A transmission sequence number.
 *
 * `u32` rather than the shipped `i32`, and the two are the same 32 bits — the shipped file holds
 * them in an `i32` and notes that *"half of them are negative to begin with"*, which is true and
 * harmless, because the only operation that matters is a wrapping subtraction. `u32` is chosen so
 * that a reader is not asked to hold *this number is negative and it does not matter*.
 */
export const struct Tsn {
  u32 v;

  /** Whether this comes before `b`, by RFC 1982. The only ordering there is. */
  bool before(const this, Tsn b) { … }

  /** `!before` at six of the ten shipped call sites; here it has a name. */
  bool atOrAfter(const this, Tsn b) { return !this.before(b); }

  /** The next one. Wrapping, which is the whole point and is the language's default. */
  Tsn next(const this) { return Tsn(this.v + 1); }

  bool eq(const this, Tsn b) { return this.v == b.v; }
}

/**
 * A stream sequence number: 16 bits, per stream, and it wraps too.
 *
 * A separate type from `Tsn` for the reason the shipped comment gives about the values being easy to
 * conflate — *"the TSN orders the association and the stream sequence orders one stream, so a peer
 * with two channels advances one TSN and two sequences"*. With both as `i32` that conflation is a
 * silent one; `takeSsn(stream)` answering a `Ssn` and `dataValue` taking one of each makes it a type
 * error.
 *
 * **Nothing in the shipped package compares two SSNs**, because ordered delivery is decided by TSN —
 * so there is no `before` here, and adding one speculatively would be inventing a rule for a case
 * that does not arise. The 16-bit wrap is real and unexercised.
 */
export const struct Ssn {
  u16 v;
  Ssn next(const this) { … }
}

// ## What could not be written
//
// **`Tsn.before` is `(a - b) < 0` and the subtraction has to wrap.** In wac it does — wrapping is the
// only arithmetic, which `../../../QUESTIONS.md` has an entry about as a *hazard*. This is the one
// place in the directory where it is the feature: the counter wraps and the comparison wraps with
// it, and a language that trapped on overflow would need a second spelling here.
//
// So the entry cuts both ways and neither half was written down. *Wrapping is the only arithmetic
// and at 64 bits there is no way to notice* is about a bug that hides; this is a correct algorithm
// that requires exactly that behaviour, in a package where the alternative would be wrong.
//
// **`u16` is a scalar here and the language has no row for it.** `../../../QUESTIONS.md` measured
// `u8` as a scalar in sixty-one places against a primitive table that lists `i8` and `i16` as *array
// element only* and has no `u8` row at all. `u16` is in the same position, and `Ssn` is the first
// one written as code here — `@/packages/zstd/src/sequences.wac` has the only other mention, inside
// a *proposed* `packed struct Entry { u8 extraBits; u8 nbBits; u16 newState; }`. One more row of the
// same table rather than a new question.
//
// **Nothing stops `Tsn(0)`.** Any 32-bit value is a legal TSN, so there is no validity check to
// write and no constructor to hide — which is the difference between this and `@/packages/ssz`'s
// `Chunk`, and is why this file is short. A wrapper that buys only the operator removal is a wrapper
// with one method, and it is still worth having.
